Congratulations on getting the whole project committed and CI-ready! That is a 
clean, solid architecture to test on bare metal.
Here is a quick Pre-Flight Hardware Test Checklist to keep handy when you boot
 your Live ISO:
• Confirm UEFI & Secure Boot State: Before running multi_distro_installer.py, 
verify ls /sys/firmware/efi/efivars returns files so mokutil and efibootmgr can
 write directly to NVRAM.
• Keep a Backup Copy of MOK.der: After installation finishes, MOK.der is placed
 on the root of your EFI partition (MOK.der) as a fallback in case MokManager 
 doesn't auto-trigger on the first reboot—you can browse to it manually via 
 Enroll key from disk in the blue MokManager menu.
• Test Snapper & rEFInd Sync Early: Once you boot into your first distro, run 
 snapper -c root list to verify Snapshot #1 is active, and try booting a second 
 distro from the rEFInd menu to confirm shared/@projects and shared/@libvirt 
 mount cleanly with UID 1000 permissions.
 Good luck with the hardware run—have fun hopping between Fedora, Debian, 
 Alpine, and your Windows 11 KVM guest!
 If you hit any hardware quirks, Secure Boot NVRAM messages, or distro-specific
 chroot edge cases during your test run, share the output here and we can patc
 them right away!
 